    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s mobile sector includes established networks such as MCI and Irancell, whose corporate relationships require careful explanation. Network availability and advertised services do not by themselves establish reliable access during national communication restrictions. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Mobile networks", "MCI", "Irancell", "Telecommunications", "Mobile internet"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:04:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mobile Operators in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "A sourced introduction to Iranian mobile operators, corporate ownership, and the distinction between mobile service and unrestricted connectivity."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "operator-identities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognizing the companies",
        "content": "Mobile operator names can describe a brand, an operating company, or a shareholder relationship. Freedom House’s 2024 assessment identifies the Mobile Telecommunication Company of Iran, abbreviated MCI, as a subsidiary of the Telecommunication Company of Iran, or TCI. It identifies MCI and Irancell as the leading mobile providers in that reporting period. These are dated descriptions rather than a verified market ranking for October 2026. [s1]\n\nIrancell’s association with South Africa’s MTN Group needs similar precision. In a July 2025 statement, MTN said that it had held a minority investment in Irancell since 2006 and that Irancell was not under its operational control. The statement is evidence of MTN’s declared corporate position; it is not an independent assessment of every aspect of Irancell’s governance. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "investment-and-control",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Investment is different from operational control",
        "content": "MTN’s statement also said that it had neither deployed capital into the Iranian business nor extracted capital or dividends from it since May 2018, following the reimposition of United States sanctions. The company described an intention, announced in 2020, to exit the Middle East in an orderly manner. An announced exit strategy should not be described as a completed disposal without evidence of completion. [s2]\n\nFor an international reader, these distinctions prevent two common misunderstandings: treating a familiar international investor as the direct operator, and treating a corporate plan as an accomplished ownership change. Neither conclusion follows from the statement inspected here. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mobile-service-during-restrictions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Service during communication restrictions",
        "content": "A mobile connection does not establish that every communication channel is available. Filterwatch’s investigation of the January 2026 shutdown reports disruption extending beyond international internet traffic to domestic infrastructure and telephone services. Its account describes some phone connections and selected online services being restored while broader restrictions persisted. [s3]\n\nThis episode illustrates why a phone’s apparent connection to a network cannot settle whether calls, messages, domestic applications, and international websites all work. The investigation supports a distinction between those services; it does not provide a complete operator-by-operator availability record. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparison-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a dependable operator comparison needs",
        "content": "An adequate comparison needs dated evidence for each provider, rather than a national headline applied to all subscribers. It should distinguish the service being tested, the location, and the period of observation. This is particularly important when restoration is selective: the return of telephone service cannot establish the return of unrestricted internet access. [s3]\n\nThis page therefore stops short of recommending a network or presenting current market shares. Its verified material explains corporate identities and a documented service limitation. A publication-ready directory still requires current primary documentation for the wider operator roster, coverage, subscriber definitions, and service terms.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
